do $do$
declare p jsonb := $pl$[{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-SV-103-v3","t":"Structure of Water and Hydrogen Bonding","s":"An astrobiologist models a hypothetical desert animal whose blood and sweat consist of a solvent whose molecules are less polar than water and form weaker hydrogen bonds with one another. Each afternoon, this animal absorbs the same solar energy as a water-based animal of equal body mass. Both animals cool off by evaporating equal masses of sweat, with all the energy for evaporation drawn from the body. Assuming all other factors are equal, which prediction about the solvent-based animal is best supported?","a":"C","c":[["A","Its body temperature rises less during afternoon heating, and each gram of sweat that evaporates removes less heat from its body.",false,"Reversed heat-capacity effect: this treats weaker hydrogen bonding as greater resistance to temperature change. Weaker attractions absorb less energy before molecules speed up, so specific heat capacity drops and temperature swings grow. Fix: Ask whether weaker bonds raise or lower the energy needed per degree of warming."],["B","Its body temperature rises more during afternoon heating, and each gram of sweat that evaporates removes more heat from its body.",false,"Evaporation ease–cooling confusion: a liquid that evaporates more easily does not remove more energy per gram. Weaker hydrogen bonds mean each gram needs less energy to escape as vapor, so less heat leaves the body. Fix: Separate how easily a liquid evaporates from the energy absorbed per gram evaporated."],["C","Its body temperature rises more during afternoon heating, and each gram of sweat that evaporates removes less heat from its body.",true,"Lower polarity means weaker hydrogen bonds, so less energy is needed to disrupt the attractions between solvent molecules. That lowers both specific heat capacity and heat of vaporization. The same energy input therefore warms the body more, and evaporating each gram absorbs less energy from the body."],["D","Its body temperature rises less during afternoon heating, and each gram of sweat that evaporates removes more heat from its body.",false,"Bond-strength–energy reversal: this assigns stronger thermal buffering and stronger evaporative cooling to weaker hydrogen bonds. Both properties depend on the energy needed to break intermolecular attractions, which decreases as those attractions weaken. Fix: Trace weaker hydrogen bonds to lower energy requirements before predicting each thermal effect."]]},{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-104","t":"Elements of Life","s":"Growing cells use phospholipids to build membranes and nucleic acids to store and transmit genetic information. The cells have enough of every required element except phosphorus. Which statement best explains how this shortage limits the production of these molecules?","a":"A","c":[["A","Production of both phospholipids and nucleic acids is limited because both contain phosphorus.",true,"Phosphorus is a required component of both phospholipids and nucleic acids. A phosphorus shortage therefore limits the cells’ ability to build membranes and produce molecules that carry genetic information."],["B","Only nucleic acid production is limited because phospholipids do not contain phosphorus.",false,"Trap: Overlooking phosphorus in phospholipids. Phospholipids also contain phosphorus, so their production is limited by the shortage. Fix: Check the required elements in both molecule types before predicting which production is limited."],["C","Only phospholipid production is limited because nucleic acids require nitrogen instead of phosphorus.",false,"Trap: Treating nitrogen and phosphorus requirements as mutually exclusive. Nucleic acids contain both elements; having enough nitrogen does not remove their phosphorus requirement. Fix: Check whether a molecule requires multiple elements rather than choosing just one."],["D","Neither type of molecule is affected because biological molecules require only carbon, hydrogen, and oxygen.",false,"Trap: Confusing the most prevalent elements with the only required elements. Carbon, hydrogen, and oxygen are abundant in biological molecules, but phospholipids and nucleic acids also require phosphorus. Fix: Identify each molecule’s required elements, not just the most abundant elements in living systems."]]},{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-SV-104-v1","t":"Elements of Life","s":"A researcher separates molecules from a sunflower seed into two purified samples: phospholipids used in membranes and nucleic acids that carry genetic information. Which prediction about phosphorus in these samples is supported by their elemental composition?","a":"D","c":[["A","Phosphorus will be present only in the nucleic acid sample because phospholipids lack phosphorus.",false,"Trap: Overlooking phosphorus in phospholipids incorrectly excludes the membrane sample. Phospholipids include phosphorus as part of their composition, so that sample also contains it. Fix: Check the elemental requirements of each molecule type before excluding a sample."],["B","Phosphorus will be present only in the phospholipid sample because nucleic acids contain nitrogen instead.",false,"Trap: Treating nitrogen and phosphorus requirements as mutually exclusive incorrectly substitutes one element for another. Nucleic acids require both nitrogen and phosphorus, so nitrogen's presence does not exclude phosphorus. Fix: Ask whether each molecule contains multiple required elements."],["C","Phosphorus will be absent from both samples because biological molecules contain only carbon, hydrogen, and oxygen.",false,"Trap: Confusing the most prevalent elements with the only required elements overlooks less abundant components. Carbon, hydrogen, and oxygen are widespread in biological molecules, but both sampled molecule types also contain phosphorus. Fix: List the required elements for each molecule rather than stopping at the most prevalent ones."],["D","Phosphorus will be present in both samples because it is a component of both molecule types.",true,"Building membrane phospholipids and information-carrying nucleic acids requires phosphorus as part of their composition. Separating these molecules into purified samples therefore does not eliminate phosphorus from either sample."]]},{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-SV-104-v2","t":"Elements of Life","s":"A waste-recycling project is evaluating discarded fish tissue as a potential phosphorus source. The tissue contains membrane-forming phospholipids and nucleic acids that carry genetic information. Based on their elemental composition, which materials could provide phosphorus for recovery?","a":"A","c":[["A","Both materials could provide phosphorus because phosphorus is part of each molecule type.",true,"Phospholipids and nucleic acids both incorporate phosphorus into their composition. Therefore, both the membrane material and the genetic-information molecules in fish tissue are potential sources of this element."],["B","Nucleic acids alone could provide phosphorus because phospholipids lack this element.",false,"Trap: Overlooking phosphorus in phospholipids incorrectly excludes the membrane material. Phosphorus is a component of phospholipids, making them a potential source as well. Fix: Check the elemental composition of each named material before excluding it."],["C","Phospholipids alone could provide phosphorus because nucleic acids contain nitrogen rather than phosphorus.",false,"Trap: Treating nitrogen and phosphorus requirements as mutually exclusive incorrectly excludes nucleic acids. Their nitrogen content does not prevent them from also containing phosphorus. Fix: Ask whether each molecule contains both elements rather than assuming one replaces the other."],["D","Neither material could provide phosphorus because both consist only of carbon, hydrogen, and oxygen.",false,"Trap: Confusing the most prevalent elements with the only required elements overlooks additional components. Carbon, hydrogen, and oxygen are common in biological molecules, but both materials also contain phosphorus. Fix: List the required elements for each material, including elements beyond carbon, hydrogen, and oxygen."]]},{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-SV-104-v3","t":"Elements of Life","s":"A gardener asks why a fertilizer for tomato plants includes phosphorus. Phospholipids form plant cell membranes, and nucleic acids carry hereditary information. Which explanation connects the fertilizer’s phosphorus to the elemental requirements of these molecules?","a":"B","c":[["A","The phosphorus provides a building material for nucleic acids alone because phospholipids lack this element.",false,"Trap: Overlooking phosphorus in phospholipids. Phospholipids contain phosphorus, so the fertilizer supplies an element required for them as well as for nucleic acids. Fix: Check the elemental requirements of each named molecule type."],["B","The phosphorus provides a building material required for both phospholipids and nucleic acids.",true,"Both molecule types include phosphorus in their composition. Supplying this element helps plants obtain the materials needed to make membrane components and molecules that carry hereditary information."],["C","The phosphorus provides a building material for phospholipids alone because nucleic acids use nitrogen instead.",false,"Trap: Treating nitrogen and phosphorus requirements as mutually exclusive. Nucleic acids require both nitrogen and phosphorus; supplying one does not replace the need for the other. Fix: Ask whether each molecule needs several different elements."],["D","The phosphorus provides no building material for either molecule because both require only carbon, hydrogen, and oxygen.",false,"Trap: Confusing the most prevalent elements with the only required elements. Carbon, hydrogen, and oxygen are common in biological molecules, but both phospholipids and nucleic acids also contain phosphorus. Fix: Distinguish an element’s abundance from whether a particular molecule requires it."]]},{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-105","t":"Elements of Life","s":"A researcher asks whether phosphorus availability limits cells’ production of DNA, which stores hereditary information. Cells can be grown in media with controlled supplies of each element. All groups start with equal numbers of identical cells and are maintained at the same temperature for the same time. Which experimental design best tests the researcher’s question?","a":"C","c":[["A","Compare DNA production in nitrogen-free and nitrogen-containing media, keeping all other elements equally available in adequate amounts.",false,"Wrong-element trap: Nitrogen is also a component of DNA, but this comparison tests nitrogen availability rather than phosphorus availability. Fix: Match the manipulated element to the element named in the research question."],["B","Compare DNA production in media lacking both phosphorus and nitrogen with media containing both, keeping all other elements equally available in adequate amounts.",false,"Confounded-variables trap: Changing phosphorus and nitrogen together prevents attributing a difference in DNA production specifically to phosphorus. Either element could limit DNA production. Fix: Change phosphorus alone while keeping nitrogen and all other elements equally available."],["C","Compare DNA production in phosphorus-free and phosphorus-containing media, keeping all other elements equally available in adequate amounts.",true,"Phosphorus is a component of DNA, so its availability can limit production of this information-storing molecule. Changing only phosphorus availability and measuring DNA production directly tests the proposed relationship."],["D","Compare phosphorus-free and phosphorus-containing media, keeping all other elements equally available in adequate amounts, and measure only phosphorus remaining in each medium.",false,"Dependent-variable mismatch: Measuring phosphorus remaining describes the medium, not how much DNA the cells produce. This measurement cannot establish whether phosphorus availability limits DNA production. Fix: Measure DNA production as the response to changing phosphorus availability."]]},{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-SV-105-v2","t":"Elements of Life","s":"A biologist wants to know whether sulfur supply limits how much protein a freshwater alga makes. The alga can be cultured in growth solutions in which the amount of each element is set by the biologist. Every culture begins with the same number of genetically identical algal cells and is kept under the same light and temperature for the same period. Which experimental design best addresses the biologist's question?","a":"A","c":[["A","Grow algae in sulfur-free and sulfur-containing solutions, supply all other elements equally and in adequate amounts, and measure the protein each culture produces.",true,"Sulfur is an element cells use to build proteins, so a shortage of sulfur could restrict protein synthesis. Varying only sulfur as the independent variable and measuring protein production as the dependent variable directly tests whether sulfur supply limits protein output."],["B","Grow algae in nitrogen-free and nitrogen-containing solutions, supply all other elements equally and in adequate amounts, and measure the protein each culture produces.",false,"Wrong-element trap: nitrogen is also part of proteins, so it seems relevant, but this design manipulates nitrogen rather than the sulfur named in the question. Its results say nothing about whether sulfur is limiting. Fix: Check that the element you change is the exact element named in the question."],["C","Grow algae in solutions lacking both sulfur and nitrogen and in solutions containing both, supply all other elements equally and in adequate amounts, and measure the protein produced.",false,"Confounded-variables trap: removing sulfur and nitrogen together means any drop in protein could be caused by either element. The effect of sulfur alone cannot be isolated. Fix: Ask whether only one variable differs between groups; change sulfur alone and hold nitrogen constant."],["D","Grow algae in sulfur-free and sulfur-containing solutions, supply all other elements equally and in adequate amounts, and measure only how much sulfur is left in each solution.",false,"Dependent-variable mismatch: leftover sulfur describes the growth solution, not the amount of protein the algae made. This measurement cannot show whether sulfur supply limits protein production. Fix: Check that the measured variable is the outcome named in the question, here protein production."]]},{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-SV-105-v3","t":"Elements of Life","s":"A microbiologist wonders whether the supply of phosphorus restricts how much phospholipid a soil bacterium makes for building its cell membranes. The bacterium can be grown in liquid cultures in which the quantity of every element is set by the investigator. Each culture is inoculated with the same number of cells from a single clone and incubated under identical temperature, oxygen, and timing conditions. Which procedure is best aligned with the microbiologist's question?","a":"A","c":[["A","Grow cultures with low phosphorus and with ample phosphorus, supply every other element equally and adequately, and measure the amount of phospholipid the cells produce.",true,"Phosphorus is a building block of phospholipids, so a shortage could cap membrane-lipid synthesis. Varying only phosphorus makes it the single independent variable, and measuring phospholipid produced is the dependent variable the question asks about."],["B","Grow cultures with low carbon and with ample carbon, supply every other element equally and adequately, and measure the amount of phospholipid the cells produce.",false,"Wrong-element trap: carbon is also in phospholipids, so it seems a reasonable variable, but this tests carbon supply rather than phosphorus supply. The results cannot answer a question about phosphorus. Fix: Check that the element you manipulate is the one named in the question."],["C","Grow cultures low in both phosphorus and carbon and cultures ample in both, supply every other element equally and adequately, and measure the amount of phospholipid the cells produce.",false,"Confounded-variables trap: changing phosphorus and carbon together means any drop in phospholipid could come from either element. The effect of phosphorus alone cannot be isolated. Fix: Vary phosphorus only while holding carbon and all other elements constant."],["D","Grow cultures with low phosphorus and with ample phosphorus, supply every other element equally and adequately, and measure only how much phosphorus is left in each culture liquid.",false,"Dependent-variable mismatch: leftover phosphorus describes the growth liquid, not how much phospholipid the cells actually built. Uptake could occur without any change in membrane-lipid output. Fix: Ask whether the measured variable is the response named in the question."]]},{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-106","t":"Elements of Life","s":"A growing cell uses nucleic acids to store and transmit genetic information and phospholipids to build membranes. Its environment supplies insufficient nitrogen and phosphorus but adequate carbon, hydrogen, oxygen, and sulfur. A researcher restores nitrogen availability to normal without changing phosphorus availability. Assuming the cell has no alternative phosphorus source, which prediction best explains the effect on production of nucleic acids and phospholipids?","a":"D","c":[["A","Nucleic acid production returns to normal, but phospholipid production remains limited because only phospholipids require phosphorus.",false,"Nitrogen-only nucleic acid trap: Recognizing nitrogen in nucleic acids does not mean phosphorus is unnecessary. Nucleic acids require both elements, so restoring only nitrogen leaves their production limited. Fix: Check every required element before predicting that a molecule’s production will recover."],["B","Phospholipid production returns to normal, but nucleic acid production remains limited because lipids require only carbon, hydrogen, and oxygen.",false,"All-lipids-are-alike trap: This applies the composition of fats to phospholipids. Phospholipids contain phosphorus, so abundant carbon, hydrogen, and oxygen cannot remove their phosphorus limitation. Fix: Identify the specific lipid type before deciding which elements it requires."],["C","Production of both nucleic acids and phospholipids returns to normal because the added nitrogen can replace phosphorus in these molecules.",false,"Element-substitution trap: Nitrogen and phosphorus are not interchangeable building materials. Adding nitrogen cannot supply the phosphorus required to construct nucleic acids and phospholipids. Fix: Match the added element to the missing element rather than total nutrient availability."],["D","Production of both nucleic acids and phospholipids remains limited because both require phosphorus.",true,"Nucleic acids require both nitrogen and phosphorus, while phospholipids also require phosphorus. Restoring nitrogen removes one shortage but leaves a shared building material insufficient, so neither type of molecule can be produced at its normal rate."]]},{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-SV-106-v1","t":"Elements of Life","s":"A laboratory produces nucleic acids that carry genetic information and phospholipids used in membranes. Two production batches have sufficient carbon, hydrogen, oxygen, and sulfur, but shortages of nitrogen and phosphorus. Batch 1 receives enough nitrogen to eliminate its nitrogen shortage. Batch 2 receives enough nitrogen and phosphorus to eliminate both shortages. Neither batch has another source of either missing element, and no other factor limits production. How should production of the two materials compare between batches?","a":"A","c":[["A","Both materials remain limited in batch 1, whereas both can reach normal production levels in batch 2.",true,"Nucleic acids need nitrogen and phosphorus, and phospholipids also need phosphorus. Batch 1 still lacks sufficient phosphorus for either material; batch 2 supplies both missing elements, removing the stated limitations."],["B","Batch 1 can produce nucleic acids normally but remains limited in phospholipid production; batch 2 can produce both normally.",false,"Nitrogen-only nucleic acid trap: This prediction treats nitrogen as the only missing element needed for nucleic acids. Their phosphorus requirement remains unmet in batch 1. Fix: List all required elements for nucleic acids before deciding whether supplementation removes their limitation."],["C","Batch 1 can produce phospholipids normally but remains limited in nucleic acid production; batch 2 can produce both normally.",false,"All-lipids-are-alike trap: This prediction assumes phospholipids need only the carbon, hydrogen, and oxygen already available. Unlike fats, phospholipids require phosphorus, which remains insufficient in batch 1. Fix: Check the named lipid’s elemental requirements rather than applying the composition of fats."],["D","Both batches can produce both materials normally because the extra nitrogen in batch 1 can substitute for its missing phosphorus.",false,"Element-substitution trap: This prediction treats different elements as interchangeable raw materials. Nitrogen cannot replace the phosphorus needed to build either nucleic acids or phospholipids. Fix: Check whether each supplement supplies the particular element that is missing."]]}]$pl$::jsonb; r jsonb; c jsonb; v_ci uuid; v_civ uuid; n int;
begin
  if jsonb_array_length(p) <> 10 then raise exception 'chunk size'; end if;
  if exists (select 1 from app.content_items ci where ci.exam_pack_version_id='2d88ba5e-a6a3-43b8-bfae-9e5505a178a7' and ci.content_key in (select x->>'k' from jsonb_array_elements(p) x))
    then raise exception 'chunk already loaded'; end if;
  for r in select * from jsonb_array_elements(p) loop
    insert into app.content_items (exam_pack_version_id, content_key, item_type, title, status)
    values ('2d88ba5e-a6a3-43b8-bfae-9e5505a178a7', r->>'k', 'mcq', r->>'t', 'draft') returning id into v_ci;
    insert into app.content_item_versions (content_item_id, version_num, stem, stimulus, content_hash, status, review_status, canonical_answer_1)
    values (v_ci, 1, r->>'s', null, md5(r->>'k'), 'draft', 'tutor_review_pending', r->>'a') returning id into v_civ;
    for c in select * from jsonb_array_elements(r->'c') loop
      insert into app.mcq_choices (content_item_version_id, choice_key, choice_text, is_correct, rationale)
      values (v_civ, c->>0, c->>1, (c->>2)::boolean, c->>3);
    end loop;
  end loop;
  select count(*) into n from app.content_items ci join app.content_item_versions v on v.content_item_id=ci.id join app.mcq_choices m on m.content_item_version_id=v.id
   where ci.exam_pack_version_id='2d88ba5e-a6a3-43b8-bfae-9e5505a178a7' and ci.status='draft' and ci.content_key in (select x->>'k' from jsonb_array_elements(p) x);
  if n <> 40 then raise exception 'chunk 2: expected 40 choice rows, got %', n; end if;
  raise notice 'chunk 2: loaded 10 draft items';
end
$do$;
